00:03:55,800 --> 00:04:01,920
CONVERSATION 2
Hi, Tony, today we're talking about how managers can get along better with young people at work.

45
00:04:01,920 --> 00:04:06,140
We often hear young people today have a poor work ethic.

46
00:04:06,140 --> 00:04:09,220
That's not really justified though, is it?

47
00:04:09,220 --> 00:04:10,300
Not really.

48
00:04:10,300 --> 00:04:16,940
In reality, young people of this age group are as varied and multifaceted as any other generation.

49
00:04:16,940 --> 00:04:22,580
Yeah, but if you are struggling to work with young people in your workspace, what can you do?

50
00:04:22,580 --> 00:04:25,940
Well, it's essential to connect with them to learn about their values.

51
00:04:25,940 --> 00:04:33,960
It's also really important to recognize the way young people want to balance their work life with their personal life.

52
00:04:33,960 --> 00:04:39,640
So they generally don't want to defer happiness in return for career advancement?

53
00:04:39,640 --> 00:04:40,580
Absolutely.

54
00:04:40,580 --> 00:04:48,640
They are not willing to sacrifice their personal lives for their work lives and this leads people to equate that with an unwillingness to work hard.

55
00:04:48,640 --> 00:04:49,300
Right.

56
00:04:49,300 --> 00:04:52,040
But that couldn't be further from the truth, could it?

57
00:04:52,040 --> 00:05:01,900
Young people will work extremely hard during their defined work hours, but they expect to have their time off, like weekends and nights free from work.

58
00:05:01,900 --> 00:05:07,040
So how should managers go about assessing young people's productivity?

59
00:05:07,040 --> 00:05:17,000
Generally, young people believe their productivity should not be measured by the number of hours they work, since someone can work for eight hours and accomplish next to nothing of significance for the company.

60
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:23,700
Instead, young people measure their productivity levels by the quality of the work they perform.

61
00:05:23,700 --> 00:05:29,680
But we often hear that young people want to feel more than just being a part of a big machine.

62
00:05:29,680 --> 00:05:33,540
Such a perspective can be hard to discredit, can't it?

63
00:05:33,540 --> 00:05:36,520
It's important to legitimize their work.

64
00:05:36,520 --> 00:05:42,320
Young workers want to work on projects that matter to themselves, the company or the world at large.

65
00:05:42,320 --> 00:05:47,780
They must feel the need to stay working at a company whose values align with their own.

66
00:05:47,780 --> 00:05:57,760
Thanks for your advice.

67
00:05:57,760 --> 00:06:18,040
Q5. According to the woman, what view about today's young people is not really justified?

68
00:06:18,040 --> 00:06:37,980
Q6. What does the man say is important for managers to get along better with young workers?

69
00:06:37,980 --> 00:06:57,380
Q7. How do young people measure their productivity levels according to the man?

70
00:06:57,380 --> 00:07:45,980
Q8. What companies does the man think would appeal to young workers?

71
00:07:45,980 --> 00:07:56,180
PASSAGE 1
Many restaurants are trying to persuade customers to eat more sustainably, moving away from environmentally unfriendly meat towards greener dishes.

72
00:07:56,180 --> 00:08:09,500
Although it sounds counter-intuitive, restaurants which include specific vegetarian sections on their menus may be unintentionally encouraging customers to eat meat, a new study suggests.

73
00:08:09,500 --> 00:08:25,080
Behavioral scientist Linda Bacon, one of the study's authors, said, "Restaurants can have a positive impact on the environment by encouraging their customers to choose more plant-based food and less meat.

74
00:08:25,080 --> 00:08:38,480
However, our findings suggest that while certain restaurant menu designs can encourage some consumers to make pro-environmental food choices, they can have the opposite effect on others."

75
00:08:38,480 --> 00:08:47,040
The study involved 750 people, half of whom frequently ate vegetarian food and half of whom rarely ate it.

76
00:08:47,040 --> 00:08:50,140
No vegetarians were included.

77
00:08:50,140 --> 00:08:56,240
They were given different menus and asked to choose a meal as if they were eating out with friends.

78
00:08:56,240 --> 00:09:05,460
Placing vegetarian dishes in a separate section did not have a significant effect on the choices made by infrequent vegetarian food eaters.

79
00:09:05,460 --> 00:09:14,820
But it did have a notable effect on the frequent eaters, lowering their chance of picking a vegetarian option by 65%.

80
00:09:14,820 --> 00:09:32,480
The researchers also discovered that presenting a vegetarian dish as the chef's recommendation, or including a more appealing description of a non-meat meal, leads to a greater proportion of infrequent vegetarian eaters choosing a vegetarian option.

81
00:09:32,480 --> 00:09:50,760
But again, these altered menu designs backfired with those who ate vegetarian food more frequently, leading them to be less likely to choose a vegetarian dish.

82
00:09:50,760 --> 00:10:12,773
Q9. What does a new study suggest about restaurants including vegetarian sections on their menus?

83
00:10:12,773 --> 00:10:30,000
Q10. How was the study carried out on 750 participants?

84
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:50,180
Q11. How do altered menu designs affect frequent eaters of vegetarian food?

85
00:10:50,180 --> 00:11:00,520
PASSAGE 2
Nottingham Trent University and the Chinese Academy of Sciences have been trying to find a way to prevent deadly outdoor air pollution from penetrating indoors.

86
00:11:00,520 --> 00:11:13,280
They found that if pollution is frozen to around -18°C in a condensing tube, the particles gather together and fall to the bottom, allowing fresh, clean air to pass through.

87
00:11:13,280 --> 00:11:17,040
Their method was able to remove 99% of pollutants.

88
00:11:17,040 --> 00:11:26,820
It is hoped that the work could pave the way for simple modification of air conditioning units so that they can also clean polluted indoor air.

89
00:11:26,820 --> 00:11:37,880
Outdoor air pollution in cities is a global problem, whether it be haze plaguing Calcutta or poor air impacting the health of children in London schools.

90
00:11:37,880 --> 00:11:46,980
While there are some existing technologies to purify indoor air, they can be inefficient, expensive or produce harmful by-products.

91
00:11:46,980 --> 00:11:51,820
When outdoor air quality is poor, people tend to spend even more time indoors.

92
00:11:51,820 --> 00:11:58,140
But outdoor pollution also leads to indoor pollution, and people still suffer.

93
00:11:58,140 --> 00:12:04,400
It is estimated that pollution is linked to over 40,000 early deaths in Britain each year.

94
00:12:04,400 --> 00:12:18,300
Professor Gang Pan of the Chinese Academy of Sciences said, "We have shown in our experiments that simply by circulating polluted air through a small freezing chamber, we can remove most of the fine particles and gas pollutants.

95
00:12:18,300 --> 00:12:27,960
Our study makes it possible to add an air cleaner option to household appliances in areas which might experience extremely poor air conditions.

96
00:12:27,960 --> 00:12:43,780
By controlling indoor air pollution and improving air quality in this way, this work could be greatly beneficial to public health."

97
00:12:43,780 --> 00:13:05,040
Q12. What does the passage say about Nottingham Trent University and the Chinese Academy of Sciences?

98
00:13:05,040 --> 00:13:25,760
Q13. What do we learn from the passage about existing technologies to purify indoor air?

99
00:13:25,760 --> 00:13:44,700
Q14. What do people tend to do when outdoor air quality is poor?

100
00:13:44,700 --> 00:14:31,280
Q15. What did Professor Gang Pan of the Chinese Academy of Sciences say about their study?

