00:14:31,280 --> 00:14:37,420
RECORDING 1
Research recently carried out by my team suggests that fish may be self-aware.

102
00:14:37,420 --> 00:14:42,280
A certain species of fish recently passed the well-known mirror test.

103
00:14:42,280 --> 00:14:47,720
This is a test that demonstrates self-recognition in animals.

104
00:14:47,720 --> 00:14:55,620
Only a few animals, such as humans, apes, dolphins and some birds, can recognize themselves in a mirror.

105
00:14:55,620 --> 00:15:05,620
And only these animals, capable of self-recognition, will attempt to remove marks placed on their skin after noticing them in the reflection.

106
00:15:05,620 --> 00:15:09,620
This ability suggests a level of higher intelligence and awareness.

107
00:15:09,620 --> 00:15:15,400
This is not shown by children until around 18 months.

108
00:15:15,400 --> 00:15:19,040
Until now, no fish has demonstrated the ability.

109
00:15:19,040 --> 00:15:31,820
But our experiments show the fish in question will scrape up against hard surfaces to remove marks placed on its body by our researchers after viewing itself in a mirror.

110
00:15:31,820 --> 00:15:43,040
The behaviors we observe leave little doubt that this fish behaviorally fulfills all criteria of the mirror test as originally laid out.

111
00:15:43,040 --> 00:15:51,760
The fish in our study never attempted to remove the marks when no mirror was present or when the marks were just placed on the mirror.

112
00:15:51,760 --> 00:16:00,440
Unmarked fish also did not attempt to remove marks from themselves when interacting with a marked fish on the other side of a window.

113
00:16:00,440 --> 00:16:13,320
These results show either that fish are self-aware or that the mirror test, which is considered to be the gold standard test used to denote higher intelligence, may be flawed.

114
00:16:13,320 --> 00:16:26,860
What is less clear is whether these behaviors should be considered as evidence that fish are self-aware, even though in the past these same behaviors have been interpreted as self-awareness in so many other animals.

115
00:16:26,860 --> 00:16:35,480
Depending on your position, you might reject the interpretation that these behaviors in a fish satisfy passing the test at all.

116
00:16:35,480 --> 00:16:45,140
But on what objective basis can you do this when the behaviors they show are so functionally similar to those of other species that have passed the test?

117
00:16:45,140 --> 00:16:49,380
Results from our research raise more questions than answers.

118
00:16:49,380 --> 00:16:57,040
What if self-awareness develops like an onion, building layer upon layer rather than appearing all at once?

119
00:16:57,040 --> 00:17:05,140
Perhaps in order to explore self-awareness further, we should stop looking at responses to the mirror as a decisive test.

120
00:17:05,140 --> 00:17:26,080
Only with a richer theory of the self and a larger test battery will we be able to determine all of the various levels of self-awareness, including where exactly fish fit in.

121
00:17:26,080 --> 00:17:43,940
Q16. What do we learn about the well-known mirror test?

122
00:17:43,940 --> 00:18:04,020
Q17. What does the study by the speaker's team show about the fish they experimented with?

123
00:18:04,020 --> 00:18:22,260
Q18. What does the speaker say about the results of their research?

124
00:18:22,260 --> 00:18:25,200
RECORDING 2
Reading with a young child is important.

125
00:18:25,200 --> 00:18:30,020
But does it matter whether you read from an electronic book or traditional print?

126
00:18:30,020 --> 00:18:34,580
As any parent knows, small children are generally keen on screens.

127
00:18:34,580 --> 00:18:53,780
So the finding from a new study that very young children enjoy electronic books more than printed picture books may not come as a huge surprise - but the following additional findings might: both parents and children behaved differently when reading electronic versus printed picture books.

128
00:18:53,780 --> 00:18:58,520
And the children who read the electronic books learned more.

129
00:18:58,520 --> 00:19:10,160
Previous studies of young children have suggested that electronic books, which incorporate extra features such as dictionaries and images that depict story events, may have advantages over print.

130
00:19:10,160 --> 00:19:21,800
In fact, multiple studies have found that electronic books support story comprehension and vocabulary gains beyond what is provided by printed books.

131
00:19:21,800 --> 00:19:33,860
But there's also work suggesting that children sometimes invest less mental effort in learning from electronic books, and that parents talk less about content when sharing such books.

132
00:19:33,860 --> 00:19:41,920
The study we're looking at today aims to give us a better understanding of how good electronic books are for young children.

133
00:19:41,920 --> 00:19:49,780
For this study, researchers videoed children aged 17 to 26 months reading with one of their parents.

134
00:19:49,780 --> 00:19:59,980
Each parent-child pair was randomly assigned to read either two educational electronic books or two printed books with the same content.

135
00:19:59,980 --> 00:20:08,420
While the parent read the words in the printed books aloud, the electronic books had audio of voice actors reading the text.

136
00:20:08,420 --> 00:20:17,160
The researchers found that parents who read the printed books pointed more frequently to pages than parents who read the electronic books.

137
00:20:17,160 --> 00:20:20,160
But the opposite was true for the children.

138
00:20:20,160 --> 00:20:29,660
Children who read the electronic books also commented more on the content and learned more new words than the children who read printed books.

139
00:20:29,660 --> 00:20:36,020
This greater learning is ascribed to greater engagement with the electronic books.

140
00:20:36,020 --> 00:20:40,220
It's important to note that the electronic books in the study were simple.

141
00:20:40,220 --> 00:20:47,280
While they included background music, animation and sound effects, there were no other extra features.

142
00:20:47,280 --> 00:20:55,880
It's also important to note that there's research on older children, finding that electronic books don't always bolster learning.

143
00:20:55,880 --> 00:20:57,880
Parents and educators need to beware.

144
00:20:57,880 --> 00:21:22,456
Extra features in electronic books may increase children's participation with them; however, if these features don't draw attention to the educational content, they may not serve as a supportive feature.

145
00:21:22,456 --> 00:21:39,660
Q19. What finding from the new study might come as a huge surprise?

146
00:21:39,660 --> 00:22:00,020
Q20. What do we learn about previous studies of young children reading electronic books?

147
00:22:00,020 --> 00:22:21,480
Q21. What extra features should be included in electronic books to bolster children's learning?

148
00:22:21,480 --> 00:22:23,720
RECORDING 3
Let me be honest with you.

149
00:22:23,720 --> 00:22:28,480
If you're living your life full of regrets, you're wasting your time.

150
00:22:28,480 --> 00:22:33,880
Why would you go through life regretting the very decisions that made you who you are today?

151
00:22:33,880 --> 00:22:39,620
There are plenty of unfavorable decisions we've made, but does that mean we should regret them?

152
00:22:39,620 --> 00:22:40,900
No!

153
00:22:40,900 --> 00:22:46,800
I've had many moments where I sit and think about all the things I wish I could have done differently.

154
00:22:46,800 --> 00:22:53,120
However, just because things could be different, it doesn't necessarily mean they would be better.

155
00:22:53,120 --> 00:22:59,760
You don't have to continually be thinking of all your past decisions and whether or not you made the right one.

156
00:22:59,760 --> 00:23:05,060
You can't go back and change anything, so what's the point in worrying about it?

157
00:23:05,060 --> 00:23:11,920
Every single decision you've ever made, positive or negative, has made you the person you are today.

158
00:23:11,920 --> 00:23:17,380
We want to go back and change parts of our past, but what would be the benefit of that?

159
00:23:17,380 --> 00:23:22,260
We would have lost the valuable knowledge from the lessons we learned.

160
00:23:22,260 --> 00:23:27,840
There are reasons you make the choices you do, and that tells us about who you are.

161
00:23:27,840 --> 00:23:32,680
So when it comes to any decision in life, be unapologetically yourself.

162
00:23:32,680 --> 00:23:37,240
Because at the end of the day, it was you who made that decision.

163
00:23:37,240 --> 00:23:45,000
With less time spent focused on regrets, you'll finally have time to focus on the more essential things in life.

164
00:23:45,000 --> 00:23:50,520
In any situation, you grow more as a person with every decision you make.

165
00:23:50,520 --> 00:23:56,600
Life is full of lessons to learn, so we can't let regret stop us from moving forward.

166
00:23:56,600 --> 00:24:05,940
We don't know if we've made the right or wrong choice until after it's already been made, so it can be easy to start regretting your decision.

167
00:24:05,940 --> 00:24:11,120
But if the decision has already been made, what's the purpose of dwelling on it?

168
00:24:11,120 --> 00:24:12,780
There isn't one.

169
00:24:12,780 --> 00:24:15,360
All you can do is move forward.

170
00:24:15,360 --> 00:24:19,240
You can't let yourself sit there and worry about every past choice.

171
00:24:19,240 --> 00:24:21,960
We aren't all perfect.

172
00:24:21,960 --> 00:24:29,500
Whenever you make a decision, there will always be a possibility for regret later, but that's how life is supposed to be.

173
00:24:29,500 --> 00:24:37,620
You will make poor decisions in life - we all do - but it's what you do with the lessons learned that matters.

174
00:24:37,620 --> 00:24:40,200
Think about every decision you've ever made.

175
00:24:40,200 --> 00:24:41,740
Were they always the best?

176
00:24:41,740 --> 00:24:42,940
Of course not.

177
00:24:42,940 --> 00:24:47,540
But look at how far you've come since then.

178
00:24:47,540 --> 00:24:55,180
**Questions:**

179
00:24:55,180 --> 00:25:13,840
Q22. What should we do with the unfavorable decisions we've made?

180
00:25:13,840 --> 00:25:32,960
Q23. What does the speaker say about every single decision we've ever made?

181
00:25:32,960 --> 00:25:51,220
Q24. What is life full of according to the speaker?

182
00:25:51,220 --> 00:26:12,408
Q25. What is said to be of real importance when we make poor decisions in life?
